{
  "passage": "About 4,700 years ago, some of the earliest cities in the world grew up along the river Indus and its tributaries. These are called the Harappan cities, after Harappa, the first site of this civilisation to be discovered, in present-day Pakistan. Other important cities included Mohenjodaro, Lothal and Dholavira. Many of these cities were divided into two parts. The western part was smaller but higher and is called the citadel, while the eastern part was larger but lower. The houses were built of baked bricks of a standard size, and they were one or two storeys high, with rooms built around a courtyard. The cities had carefully planned drains, covered with stones, running along the streets. Each house's drain was connected to these street drains. Dholavira, on the island of Khadir in the Rann of Kutch in Gujarat, is remarkable for its water storage. Its people built large reservoirs cut into rock to collect rainwater, because the region was dry. Archaeologists also found a large signboard there, with letters of the Harappan script set in white stone. Lothal, in Gujarat, had a dockyard where boats could be loaded and unloaded, and a workshop for making beads from precious stones. The Harappan script has not yet been read, so much of what we know comes from the remains of buildings, pots, seals and tools.",
